<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.8.3: Understanding That the Quadratic Formula Is the Combined Steps of Completing the Square</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02466</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.8.3: Understanding That the Quadratic Formula Is the Combined Steps of Completing the Square</md:title>
    <md:uuid>15324b4e-953c-4b45-989c-71ab982a99ea</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Cool Down Activity</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Here is one solution for solving \(x^2+3x+8=0\) by <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">completing the square</term>, where each step is shown, but numerical expressions are not evaluated.</para>
          <para id="para-00002">Original equation \(x^2+3x+8=0\)</para>
          <para id="para-00003"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - \(x^2+3x=-8\)</para>
          <para id="para-00004"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - \(4x^2+4(3x)=4(-8)\)</para>
          <para id="para-00005"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - \((2x)^2+6(2x)=-32\)</para>
          <para id="para-00006"><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - \(P^2+6P=-32\)</para>
          <para id="para-00007"><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - \(P^2+6P+3^2=-32+3^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00008"><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - \((P+3)^2=3^2-32\)</para>
          <para id="para-00009"><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - \(P+3= \pm \sqrt{3^2-32}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00010"><emphasis>Step 8</emphasis> - \(P=-3 \pm \sqrt{3^2-32}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00011"><emphasis>Step 9</emphasis> - \(2x=-3 \pm \sqrt{3^2-32}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00012"><emphasis>Step 10</emphasis> - \(x= \frac{-3 \pm \sqrt{3^2-32}}{2}\)</para>
          <para id="para-00013">In Step 2, the equation is multiplied by 4. Why might that be?</para>
          <para id="para-00014">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00016">To make the coefficient of \(x^2\) a <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00002">perfect square</term> and the coefficient of \(x\) an even number</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00017">In Step 5, \(3^2\) is added to each side. Why might that be?</para>
          <para id="para-00018">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00019">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00020">\(3^2\) completes the square for the expression on the left side. To keep the two sides equal, it needs to be added to the other side as well.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00021">What happened between Step 5 and Step 6?</para>
          <para id="para-00022">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00023">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00024">The perfect square is rewritten in factored form, and \(-32+3^2\) is rewritten as \(3^2-32\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <note id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00025">Building Character: Intellectual Humility</para>
  <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_10_10_08_01.png"/>
          </media>

            <para id="para-00026">No matter how old you are, with intellectual humility you become wiser. It helps you be less judgmental of others, learn more in school, and be a better leader.</para>
            <para id="para-00027">Think about yourself. Are these statements true for you?</para>
            <list id="list-00001">
          <item>I recognize the value in opinions that are different from my own. </item>
          <item>I accept that my beliefs and attitudes may be wrong. </item>
        </list>
            <para id="para-00028">Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>